--- body ---
## Context

The first child, and the only one carrying design risk: everything after it
depends on the schema and on how `SqliteSearchIndex` relates to the existing
class. Tracker: 0001.

## Decisions taken (reviewed 2026-08-21)

**Schema — a plain FTS5 table plus an indexed side table.** Not external
content, and not FTS5 alone. An `UNINDEXED` FTS5 column is not indexed, so a
per-item lookup is a full scan: measured on the 408 628-passage bench,
**362 ms by item against 0 ms for an indexed MATCH**. Ticket 0006 deletes by
item on every delta, so a fifty-item delta would cost eighteen seconds.

    CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE passages USING fts5(
      body, tokenize='unicode61 remove_diacritics 2');
    CREATE TABLE passage_meta(
      rowid INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,   -- = passages.rowid
      item TEXT NOT NULL, ord INTEGER NOT NULL,
      title TEXT, source TEXT      -- NULL for metadata, 'fulltext' otherwise
    );
    CREATE INDEX passage_meta_item ON passage_meta(item);

FTS5 carries only the text; everything else sits in an ordinary table with a
real index on `item`. That side table is also where 0004's vector BLOB goes.

**Tokenizer** `unicode61 remove_diacritics 2`, verified to fold accents. No
stemming: FTS5's `porter` is English-only and the corpus is not. Do **not**
add `prefix='2 3'` here — it would make prefix queries cheap, and prefix is the
only morphology available in French, but zoteus issues no prefix queries today,
so enabling it changes behaviour nothing asked to change.

**Query sanitisation is a named piece of work, not a detail.** `MATCH` takes a
query *language*: `"`, `*`, `NEAR`, `OR`, `-`, `(` are syntax there. zoteus's
`BM25Index.search(q)` receives the raw string and tokenises it itself, so any
input is safe today. Passing `args.q` to `MATCH` would turn a user query
containing an apostrophe into a SQLite syntax error surfaced as a tool failure.
Tokenise the query with the same tokenizer as the index, quote each term, join
with `OR` — zoteus's BM25 ORs its terms, while FTS5 defaults to an implicit AND,
so this also settles the semantics.

**Keep `makeSnippet`, do not adopt FTS5's `snippet()`.** `query()` calls
`makeSnippet(rec.text, q)` and so needs the passage text at query time; return
`body` from the row. FTS5's `snippet()` is better — it knows token offsets
rather than searching for a substring — and that is exactly why it is out of
scope here: it would change the output and break the same-results-out
comparison that is this chantier's load-bearing gate. Switching to it later is
a deliberate, separate change.

**File location** `<ZOTEUS_DATA_DIR>/search-index.sqlite`, beside
`search-index.json`, so both coexist during 0005.

**Shadow, do not replace.** `SqliteSearchIndex` is a second implementation
selected by config, defaulting to the existing path. Without both live at once
there is nothing to compare, and the comparison is the gate.

**Shape: a passage-store port, not a duplicated class** (settled 2026-08-21,
raid round). Read literally, "a second implementation" duplicates some 600
lines of `SearchIndex` -- `build`, `buildIncremental`, the embedder plumbing,
`status` -- when only the storage differs. Instead the passage store is
extracted behind a small internal port and injected:

    interface PassageStore {
      add(rec: ChunkRecord): void
      search(q: string, k: number): Array<{ id: string; score: number }>
      get(id: string): ChunkRecord | undefined
      deleteByItem(item: string): void
      clear(): void
      readonly size: number
    }

`MemoryPassageStore` wraps today's `BM25Index` + `chunks` Map and is the
default, so the existing path is unchanged by construction.
`Fts5PassageStore` is the SQLite one. `SqliteSearchIndex extends SearchIndex`
and injects it, which keeps the ticket's shadow-do-not-replace posture and the
config-selected name. Three things this buys: upstream's tests run unchanged
through a factory swap, the same-results-out gate isolates the *store* as the
only variable rather than comparing two whole implementations, and 0004's
vector BLOB and 0006's delete-by-item get a declared home on the port.

**The engines floor stays at `>=20.19`** (settled 2026-08-21). `node:sqlite`
lands in Node 22.5 -- verified here on v22.23.1: FTS5 present, sqlite 3.51.3,
`unicode61 remove_diacritics 2` folds `eleve`/`élève`, and an
`ExperimentalWarning` goes to stderr, which is harmless for an MCP server whose
protocol channel is stdout. The SQLite path is opt-in and off by default, so a
Node 20 install never reaches it. Import `node:sqlite` lazily inside
`Fts5PassageStore` and throw a message naming the version requirement when it
is absent -- never a silent fall back to the JS index, which would hand the
user the OOM they were escaping with nothing saying so. Bumping `engines` is a
packaging question for whenever this becomes a merge request, not for 0002.

**"Match" means top-k item-set overlap, not score equality** (settled
2026-08-21). FTS5's `bm25()` uses its own parameters, its own tokenizer and no
stopword list, so scores cannot agree and exact rank equality would fail for
reasons that are not defects. The gate: on a fixture corpus and a fixed query
list, every query's top-3 `itemKey`s are a subset of the JS index's top-5, and
no query returns empty where the other returns hits. That tests retrieval
equivalence, which is what a user feels.

## Relevant files

- `fork/src/features/search/index-manager.ts` — the `SearchIndex` interface to satisfy
- `fork/src/features/search/bm25.ts`, `tokenize.ts` — what FTS5 replaces
- `bench/fts5_bench.mjs` — the standalone prototype the schema comes from

## Actions

1. Define `PassageStore` and `MemoryPassageStore` in a new
   `fork/src/features/search/passage-store.ts`; move `ChunkRecord` there (or
   re-export) so both stores and `index-manager.ts` share one definition.
2. Add `store?: PassageStore` to `SearchIndexOptions`; replace `this.bm25` and
   `this.chunks` in `index-manager.ts` with `this.store`, defaulting to
   `MemoryPassageStore`. `reset()` calls `store.clear()`. No behaviour change:
   the existing suite must stay green before any SQLite code is written.
3. Write `fork/src/features/search/fts5-store.ts` -- the DDL from the ticket
   body, a lazy `node:sqlite` import, `PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL`, and a prepared
   insert into `passages` + `passage_meta`.
4. Write the query sanitiser: tokenise with the index's own rules, quote each
   term, join with `OR`. Its own unit test covers apostrophes, `"`, `*`,
   `NEAR`, `-`, `(`, and the empty-query case.
5. `SqliteSearchIndex extends SearchIndex` in
   `fork/src/features/search/sqlite-index.ts`, taking `dbPath` and defaulting
   to `<ZOTEUS_DATA_DIR>/search-index.sqlite`.
6. Parameterise upstream's search tests over a store factory so the same
   bodies run against both, using `:memory:` for the SQLite pass.
7. Add the top-k overlap comparison test described above.

## Test

Red step: run upstream's existing search tests against `SqliteSearchIndex`
instead of `SearchIndex`. They fail on the unimplemented methods first, then
pass as each lands. No new test fixtures — reusing upstream's is the point.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `SqliteSearchIndex` satisfies the `SearchIndex` interface, keyword-only
- [ ] The default (memory) path is unchanged: upstream's suite green before any
      SQLite code exists
- [ ] Upstream's suite passes against it
- [ ] Query semantics match the current BM25 on a shared sample of queries:
      every query's top-3 items a subset of the JS index's top-5, no query empty
      on one side and non-empty on the other
- [ ] `tsc --noEmit` and `eslint` clean
